      "text": "We want to find the maximum value of the expression \\(\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2}\\). To do this, we can use algebraic manipulation and the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality.\n\nFirst, expand the numerator:\n\\[\n(x + y)^2 = x^2 + 2xy + y^2.\n\\]\nThus, the expression becomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2 + 2xy + y^2}{x^2 + y^2}.\n\\]\nWe can rewrite this as:\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2 + y^2 + 2xy}{x^2 + y^2} = 1 + \\frac{2xy}{x^2 + y^2}.\n\\]\nTo maximize this expression, we need to minimize the term \\(\\frac{2xy}{x^2 + y^2}\\). By the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, we know that:\n\\[\n(x^2 + y^2)(1^2 + 1^2) \\geq (x + y)^2 \\implies 2(x^2 + y^2) \\geq (x + y)^2 \\implies x^2 + y^2 \\geq \\frac{(x + y)^2}{2}.\n\\]\nEquality holds when \\(\\frac{x}{1} = \\frac{y}{1}\\), so \\(x = y\\). Substituting \\(x = y\\) into the expression, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2} = \\frac{(x + x)^2}{x^2 + x^2} = \\frac{(2x)^2}{2x^2} = \\frac{4x^2}{2x^2} = 2.\n\\]\nTherefore, the maximum value of \\(\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2}\\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
